Yes\'97but with an important distinction:\
\
## Global origin\
\
The original **Computer Clubhouse** began in **1993 at the Computer Museum in Boston, Massachusetts**, now part of the Museum of Science, Boston. It was created through a collaboration between the museum and the **MIT Media Lab**, particularly MIT researcher Mitchel Resnick and educator Natalie Rusk. [media.mit](https://www.media.mit.edu/projects/computer-clubhouse/overview/)\
\
So, it is accurate to say that the project began **in collaboration with MIT**, but the first physical Clubhouse was **not located at MIT**. It was located at Boston\'92s Computer Museum. The program grew out of earlier robotics and creative-technology work connected with the MIT Media Lab. [web.media.mit](https://web.media.mit.edu/~mres/papers/Clubhouse/clubhouse-origins.pdf)\
\
## Panama\'92s beginning\
\
Panama joined the international Clubhouse initiative in **2004**, initially with two centers:\
\
- **Parroquia de F\'e1tima**, in El Chorrillo.\
- **Fe y Alegr\'eda**, in El Ingenio.\
\
These were the first Panamanian Clubhouse sites. [clab](https://clab.school/en/blog/mitchel-resnick-the-mind-behind-scratch/)\
\
The later **Rincones Clubhouse** national program is a separate phase of development led by Panama\'92s **SENACYT**, in partnership with organizations including the Boston Museum of Science. Its pilot phase began in **2017** at Infoplaza UNESCO in La Chorrera and Infoplaza San Pancracio in San Miguelito. [senacyt.gob](https://www.senacyt.gob.pa/wp-content/uploads/2019/06/MEMORIA-SENACYT-2018.pdf)\
\
In short: **Boston, 1993, was the origin of the global Clubhouse model; Panama adopted it in 2004; and SENACYT relaunched and expanded the \'93Rincones Clubhouse\'94 model through a national pilot beginning in 2017.**}